1️⃣ Heap is divided into many small regions

Instead of one big Young and one big Old area,
G1GC breaks the heap into hundreds of small regions (1MB–32MB each).
Each region can act as:

Young region
Old region
Humongous region (very large objects)



👉 This allows G1 to pick small areas to clean instead of freezing the whole heap.

2️⃣ Young GC happens first
Young GC = cleaning the new/temporary objects.
G1GC does:

Eden → Survivor → Old promotion
Dead objects are removed
Live objects are copied to Survivor/Old regions

This process is parallel and very fast.

3️⃣ G1 finds which regions have the MOST garbage
This is the main feature.
G1 = Garbage First

G1GC scans all regions
Estimates which ones contain the most garbage
Prepares a list → “These are the dirtiest regions”

👉 It focuses only on areas that give the highest garbage cleanup benefit.

4️⃣ Mixed GC — cleaning both Young + Old regions
After some time, G1GC begins Mixed GC:
It cleans:

Eden regions
Survivor regions
Some Old regions (not all)

It chooses only old regions that are most garbage-filled.
👉 This avoids long stop-the-world Full GCs.

5️⃣ Compaction happens while copying
Unlike older collectors, G1GC compacts memory automatically as it copies objects.
When live objects are moved:

They are placed together (no holes)
The region becomes empty and reusable

👉 This prevents fragmentation without long pauses.

🧠 What about Full GC?
G1GC tries very hard to avoid Full GC.
But a Full GC may happen if:

Humongous objects are too many
Not enough free regions
Promotion failures happen
Tuning/memory too small

In Full GC, JVM pauses everything and does a classic mark-sweep-compact, but it’s rare.

🎯 Super Simple Summary
Here’s G1GC in 5 easy lines:

Breaks heap into many small regions
Cleans young regions normally
Finds regions with maximum garbage
Cleans only those regions (Young + Old) → Mixed GC
Copies + compacts objects while cleaning.

ZGC:
====
ZGC is a scalable, low‑latency garbage collector introduced in Java 11 and improved in later versions.
Its main goal:
👉 Pause times always remain below 10 milliseconds, even with very large heaps.

GC (Z Garbage Collector) is a very low‑latency garbage collector that keeps GC pauses always under 1–2 milliseconds, even if your heap is huge (GBs to TBs).
✔ It does almost all garbage collection while your application is running
✔ It compacts memory (moves objects) without stopping the application
✔ It uses smart tricks like colored pointers and load barriers.

STEP 1: Heap is divided into regions
ZGC divides the heap into many small parts called regions.
Think of the heap as many small boxes instead of one big block.
This makes it easy to clean and move objects in small pieces.

STEP 2: ZGC uses colored pointers
Every object reference (pointer) contains extra bits.
These extra bits tell ZGC:

Whether the object is marked
Whether it is being moved
Whether the pointer is old and needs fixing

This is revolutionary because ZGC doesn’t have to stop your program to check states.

STEP 3: Load Barriers — the most important part
Whenever your program reads an object, ZGC intercepts that read:
👉 It checks whether the object was moved
👉 If moved, ZGC silently fixes the pointer to point to the new location
👉 And your program continues normally
This allows ZGC to move objects without stopping the application.

STEP 4: ZGC Cycle Phases (Very Easy)
Phase A: Mark Start (tiny pause <1ms)
ZGC pauses all threads just long enough to see what objects are reachable from roots.
This pause is extremely small.

Phase B: Concurrent Marking
ZGC now scans the heap to find all reachable objects.
🔹 Your application continues running
🔹 ZGC continues marking
🔹 Load barriers fix pointers when reads happen
So this phase is fully concurrent.

Phase C: Mark End (tiny pause <1ms)
A very small pause to finalize marking.

Phase D: Select which regions to relocate
ZGC finds:

Which regions are mostly empty
Which ones are fragmented
Which objects should be moved

Still no pause.

Phase E: Concurrent Relocation (Compaction)
This is the killer feature.
ZGC moves objects to new locations while:

Your threads are running
Your code is accessing them
Load barriers keep fixing pointers

So compaction becomes completely concurrent.
No other GC compacts this way.

Phase F: Concurrent Reset
ZGC clears metadata and prepares for next cycle.
Still concurrent.





1. Purpose

G1 GC: Designed for low pause times while still achieving good throughput. Ideal for large heaps (multi-GB) but not extreme sizes.
ZGC: Designed for ultra-low pause times (sub-millisecond) even with very large heaps (up to terabytes).


✅ 2. Heap Size Support

G1 GC: Efficient for heaps up to tens of GBs.
ZGC: Scales to multi-terabyte heaps without increasing pause times.


✅ 3. Pause Times

G1 GC: Pause times are typically in the range of 10–100 ms, depending on tuning and heap size.
ZGC: Pause times are always below 10 ms, often sub-millisecond, regardless of heap size.


✅ 4. Algorithm

G1 GC: Region-based collector; divides heap into regions and prioritizes garbage collection based on "garbage first" principle.
ZGC: Uses colored pointers and load barriers for concurrent relocation, making most work concurrent with application threads.


✅ 5. Concurrency

G1 GC: Mostly concurrent, but still has stop-the-world phases for marking and cleanup.
ZGC: Almost fully concurrent; minimal stop-the-world phases (usually under 1 ms).


✅ 6. Use Cases

G1 GC: Good for server-side applications with moderate heap sizes and predictable pause requirements.
ZGC: Best for large-scale applications, in-memory databases, or real-time systems where ultra-low latency is critical.


✅ 7. Availability

G1 GC: Default GC in many modern JVMs (Java 9+).
ZGC: Available since Java 11, improved in later versions.


